< \section{Processors for Embedded Systems}
< %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
< \nsl{Embedded systems are application which needs a special processor mostly. A standard PC isn't suitable because the microprocessor which is used doesn't work alone. You have to spend motherboard mostly of a big size and big costs so that you get a running system.
< \newline
< On the other hand when you are looking on microcontroller application you will find that especially the 8-bit controllers have to small computer power and to hard restrictions in building appropriate internal or external memories so that these processors are also not suitable for a higher sophisticated embedded system. The mentioned restrictions are not so hard if you are working with 16- and 32-bit microcontrollers. A big advantage of microcontrollers is that nearly every interface is available which we have to use in the case of embedded systems.
< \newline
< If in an application you could spend a small motherboard (no hard discs, no graphical user interfaces mostly) than it makes sense to look for an ``embedded PC'' or a PC/104 system. Such systems contain a special so called ``mobile microprocessor'' like ``Pentium M'' or ``Geode'' or ``XScale''. These processors are variants of a standard 8086-like microprocessor (Pentium M and Geode). If you are working with these embedded PC's than you can get the support from the well known standard PC tools like compilers, linkers and operating systems. 
< \newline
< The last type of processor which could be involved in an embedded system is a signal processor. A signal processor has much more computer power as a microcontroller. But in contrary to a microprocessor a signal processor has nearly the same interfaces like a microcontroller. The disadvantage of the use of a signal processor is that there aren't such development tools.
< }
< 
