> \item[{\rot\bf Onchip memory }]~\\[-3ex]
> \bi
> 	\ite Onchip SRAM
> 	\bi 
> 		\ite organized in two blocks as 4048 x 32 bits.
> 		\ite the RAM base address registers (RAMBAR0 and RAMBAR1) define the logical base address attributes, and access types for the two SRAM modules.
> 		\ite byte, word, and longword address capabilities.
> 		\ite single-cycle throughput (the fastest memory after the registers).
> 	\ei
> 	\bigskip
> 	\ite Cache
> 	\bi
> 		\ite  is organized as instruction cache (read cache, size 32 Kbyte) and data cache (read/write cache, 32 Kbyte).
> 		\ite two parallel accesses possible (Harvard-architecture).
> 		\ite works with the core-frequency.
> 		\ite cache memory isn't direct accessible (controlled by the cache-controller).
> 		\ite the use of a cache doesn't allow to specify a precise execution time.
> 	\ei 
> \ei
> \os{\newpage}
> \begin{center}
> \epsfig{file=figures/Fig_3_13_Cache,width=0.9\textwidth}\\
> Fig 3.13 Blockdiagram of the Data-Cache
> \end{center}
> \os{\newpage}
> \item[{\rot\bf External memory }]~\\[-3ex]
> \bi 
> 	\ite FlexBus:
> 	\bi 
> 		\ite multiplexed external 32-bit bus.
> 		\ite spends six independent, user-programmable chip-select signals (FBCS[5:0]).
> 		\ite generates an interface for SRAM, PROM, EPROM, EEPROM, Flash, and other peripherals.
> 		\ite allows the use of 8-, 16-, and 32-bit port sizes (transfer).
> 		\ite transfer programmable for burst and burst-inhibited transfers (selectable for each chip select and transfer).
> 		\ite timing behaviour programmable (access times for chip selects).
> 	\ei
> 
> \ei
> 
> \os{\newpage}
> \begin{center}
> \epsfig{file=figures/Fig_3_14_External_Memory_Port,width=0.9\textwidth}\\
> Fig 3.14 Connections for external Port Sizes
> \end{center}
> 
> \begin{center}
> \epsfig{file=figures/Fig_3_15_FlexBus,width=0.9\textwidth}\\
> Fig 3.15 FlexBus
> \end{center}
> \bi 
> 	\ite SDRAM:
> 	\bi 
> 		\ite separate controller which generates the necessary signals and functions to drive SDRAM.
> 		\ite 32-bit fixed memory port width.
> 		\ite built 64-bit data bus interface to internal bus.
> 		\ite supports page mode to maximize the data rate.
> 		\ite supports sleep mode and self-refresh mode.
> 		\ite maximum memory size 1 GByte.
> 		\ite programmable ``memory base address'' register to place the SDRAM on a specific position inside the address space. 
> 	\ei
> \ei
> 
