> \epsfig{file=figures/Fig_3_16_SDRAM_Controller,width=0.9\textwidth}\\
> Fig 3.16 SDRAM Controller Blockdiagram
> \end{center}
> 
> \item[{\rot\bf Interrupt Controller }]~\\[-3ex]
> \bi 
> 	\ite handles all interrupts and exceptions.
> 	\ite interupts \pr external inputs with a programmable interrupt-level (level 1 lowest level, 7 highest level).
> 	\ite all interrupts and exceptions work with an appropriate interrupt vector.
> 	\ite the interrupt vector will be read in in the interrupt acknowledge cycle (1 byte value).
> 	\ite if an interrupt or exception was acknowledged the core generates a stack frame (which holds contents of the CCR-register and the program counter PC) and starts with the execution of the interrupt service routine. 
> 	\ite the start address of the interrupt service routine are placed in the interrupt vector table (contains of the interrupt vector base register + 4 * value of the interrupt vector).
> 	\ite the external interrupts could be enabled and disabled.
> \ei
> 
> \os{\newpage}
> 
> \item[{\rot\bf BDM (\underline{b}ackground \underline{d}ebugger \underline{m}ode) }]~\\[-3ex]
> \bi 
> 	\ite an additional interface for debugging.
> 	\ite allows the download and test of programs without using any other support (no monitor programs or bootstrap loader).
> 	\ite the BDM mode could be started with a hardware switch.
> 	\ite real-time trace support (the ability to determine the dynamic execution path through an application).
> 	\ite the BDM interface is 8-bit parallel output bus that reports processor execution status and data to an external BDM emulator system.
> 	\ite in the BDM mode the processor is halted and a variety of commands can be sent to the processor to access memory and registers.
> \ei
